--TEST--
a contagious parameter still refuses a tainted argument when its answer is an instance, a `mixed` or an `array<mixed>` — the half of the admission that is deliberately conservative, agreed across four classes — `rule:security/unclassified-parameter-refuses-tainted`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `nvs_types::expr::quals::admits_tainted_argument`'s doc comment owns why:
// admitting an argument the answer cannot carry the qualifier out of would
// launder it, so the refusal stays until there is somewhere to put the bit.
// Every member below is `Qual::Contagious` in the parameter that refuses --
// the classification is not what stops these, the return type is, which is
// what makes this an agreement between four classes rather than four rows.
tainted string $t = "a";
tainted bytes $tb = "ab" as bytes;

// An instance answer: `Core\Uri` has no qualifier to carry.
Core\Uri $uri = Core\Uri::parse($t);

// A `mixed` answer: `rule:types/declaration`'s one unchecked position is exactly where a
// qualifier would be lost.
mixed $decoded = Core\Json::decode($t);

// An `array<mixed>` answer -- the element is the place a qualifier would go,
// and `mixed` is not a place.
array<mixed> $fields = Core\Bytes::unpack($tb, "C2");

// A nullable instance: a union whose members are an instance and `null`, so
// neither member can carry it either.
?Core\Regex\Match $matched = Core\Regex::match($t, "a");
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:12:33
%A
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:16:36
%A
error[E0401]: expected `bytes`, found `tainted bytes`
   --> case.nvs:20:43
%A
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:24:48
%A
